---
// The screening decision: consequence of failure sets the class; a catastrophic part is fracture-critical unless it
// is shown to be fail-safe, and a pressure vessel always is. What each class drives.
const ink = 'var(--rd-ink)', muted = 'var(--rd-muted)', accent = 'var(--rd-accent)', warn = 'var(--rd-warn)', ok = 'var(--rd-ok)';
const cols = [
  { x: 8, w: 120, q: 'Catastrophic', cls: 'Class A', c: accent, drive: ['A-basis · 100% NDT', 'independent check'] },
  { x: 136, w: 96, q: 'Mission loss', cls: 'Class B', c: warn, drive: ['A/B-basis · NDT', 'design authority'] },
  { x: 240, w: 112, q: 'Degraded or none', cls: 'Class C', c: ok, drive: ['B-basis · sample', 'normal release'] },
];
---

<figure class="viz critflow">
  <p class="viz-title">Classify by consequence</p>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 360 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="critflow-desc" style="max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <desc id="critflow-desc">
      A decision chart. The question is what happens if the part fails. A catastrophic failure makes the part Class A;
      it is fracture-critical unless it is fail-safe, and a pressure vessel always is. A
      loss of the mission makes it Class B. A degraded performance or no effect makes it Class C. Class A needs A-basis
      allowables, 100% NDT and an independent check; Class B needs A- or B-basis allowables, NDT and design-authority
      approval; Class C needs B-basis allowables, sample inspection and the normal release route.
    </desc>
    <defs>
      <marker id="cf-arr" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="context-stroke"></path></marker>
    </defs>
    <rect x="96" y="10" width="168" height="28" rx="5" fill="var(--rd-panel)" stroke={ink}></rect>
    <text x="180" y="28" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" fill={ink}>What happens if it fails?</text>
    {cols.map((c) => (
      <g>
        <path d={`M180,38 L${c.x + c.w / 2},62`} fill="none" stroke={muted} marker-end="url(#cf-arr)"></path>
        <text x={c.x + c.w / 2} y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill={ink}>{c.q}</text>
        <line x1={c.x + c.w / 2} x2={c.x + c.w / 2} y1="84" y2="104" stroke={muted} marker-end="url(#cf-arr)"></line>
        <rect x={c.x} y="106" width={c.w} height="28" rx="5" fill={c.c} fill-opacity="0.7"></rect>
        <text x={c.x + c.w / 2} y="124" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="600" fill={ink}>{c.cls}</text>
        <text x={c.x + c.w / 2} y="232" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" fill={ink}>{c.drive[0]}</text>
        <text x={c.x + c.w / 2} y="245" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" fill={muted}>{c.drive[1]}</text>
      </g>
    ))}
    <line x1="68" x2="68" y1="134" y2="150" stroke={accent} marker-end="url(#cf-arr)"></line>
    <rect x="8" y="152" width="120" height="62" rx="5" fill="none" stroke={accent} stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect>
    <text x="68" y="166" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" fill={accent}>fracture-critical</text>
    <text x="68" y="179" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" fill={accent}>unless fail-safe;</text>
    <text x="68" y="192" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" fill={accent}>a pressure vessel</text>
    <text x="68" y="205" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9.5" fill={accent}>always is</text>
    <line x1="4" x2="356" y1="220" y2="220" stroke="var(--rd-line)"></line>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>
    A generic scheme: your programme's classes and their names will differ, but the logic is the same. The class is set
    by the consequence, not by how hard the part is to make. It sets the allowable basis, the inspection and who signs
    the drawing.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
